<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saladin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1193</span> · Also known as Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub, Salah al-Din</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saladin (Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub) was a Kurdish Muslim military leader and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, who died in 1193. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1169, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and united Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under his rule. His victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187 and recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> made him a celebrated figure, though the Ayyubid Empire profile notes that his division of territory among relatives prepared the empire's fragmentation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group (CNG) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
